{"causes": ["The election of Pope Urban VI in 1378 created immediate instability within the College of Cardinals, leading to the Western Schism.", "Pope Urban VI's increasingly autocratic and volatile behavior alienated many cardinals, including Giovanni d'Amelia.", "The ongoing political rivalries among European powers exacerbated the divisions within the Catholic Church during the late 14th century."], "effects": ["Giovanni d'Amelia's execution by Pope Urban VI further intensified the political and religious divisions of the Western Schism.", "His death contributed to the perception of Urban VI as a tyrannical leader, hindering efforts to heal the schism.", "The incident served as a stark reminder of the extreme risks involved in challenging papal authority during the late Medieval period."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "urban-vi", "sourceName": "Pope Urban VI", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "giovanni-da-amelia", "targetName": "Giovanni da Amelia", "context": "Pope Urban VI initially elevated Giovanni d'Amelia to cardinal in 1378, but his subsequent tyranny led d'Amelia to conspire against him."}, {"sourceSlug": "giovanni-da-amelia", "sourceName": "Giovanni da Amelia", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "western-schism", "targetName": "Western Schism", "context": "Cardinal Giovanni d'Amelia was a key figure in the early years of the Western Schism, initially supporting Urban VI before his involvement in a plot against him."}, {"sourceSlug": "giovanni-da-amelia", "sourceName": "Giovanni da Amelia", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "kingdom-of-naples", "targetName": "Kingdom of Naples", "context": "Giovanni d'Amelia was arrested along with other cardinals in Nocera, then part of the Kingdom of Naples, in 1385 after their plot against Pope Urban VI."}, {"sourceSlug": "giovanni-da-amelia", "sourceName": "Giovanni da Amelia", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "papal-intrigue", "targetName": "Papal Intrigue", "context": "Giovanni d'Amelia's conspiracy against Pope Urban VI, leading to his execution, epitomizes the intense papal intrigue of the late 14th century."}, {"sourceSlug": "clement-vii-antipope", "sourceName": "Antipope Clement VII", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "western-schism", "targetName": "Western Schism", "context": "The election of Antipope Clement VII directly contributed to the Western Schism, setting the stage for Giovanni d'Amelia's involvement in papal politics."}], "places": [{"name": "Amelia, Papal States", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Nocera Inferiore, Kingdom of Naples", "role": "Place of arrest"}, {"name": "Genoa, Republic of Genoa", "role": "Location of execution"}], "texts": [], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1772107"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Giovanni d'Amelia's execution by Pope Urban VI in 1386 was a dramatic episode that underscored the brutal power struggles within the Papacy during the Western Schism, further entrenching the schism and damaging the legitimacy of the Roman pontiff. His fate illustrates the extreme political and religious turmoil of late medieval Europe.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-23T09:18:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39930605833", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Giovanni da Amelia (b. 1309, Amelia) was a entrepreneur or business leader of the Medieval period, shaped by feudalism, religious institutions, and cultural exchange, active in Amelia. Known in the fields of religious figures.", "newValue": "Giovanni d'Amelia (b. c. 1309, Amelia), originally Giovanni di Bartolomeo, emerged as a prominent Italian cardinal during the tumultuous late 14th century, a period defined by the nascent Western Schism and profound ecclesiastical upheaval. Elevated to the cardinalate by Pope Urban VI in 1378, d'Amelia's career spanned a critical juncture where the authority of the Roman pontiff was fiercely contested, and loyalty to the Holy See was fraught with political and personal peril. His trajectory, from a skilled canon lawyer to a cardinal involved in papal intrigue, illuminates the intense power struggles and shifting alliances that characterized the Avignonese Papacy's aftermath and the subsequent schism.

Initially a staunch supporter of Pope Urban VI following the controversial election of 1378, Cardinal d'Amelia was instrumental in the early attempts to consolidate Urban's authority against the Avignon anti-pope Clement VII. He served as a papal legate, undertaking diplomatic missions that sought to secure allegiances for the Roman line. However, the increasingly autocratic and volatile nature of Urban VI, particularly his treatment of the College of Cardinals, sowed dissent among his closest advisors. D'Amelia, along with several other cardinals, became embroiled in a conspiracy to depose Urban VI, culminating in their arrest at Nocera in 1385.

The consequence of d'Amelia's involvement in the plot against Urban VI was swift and brutal. Following torture, he was executed on January 27, 1386, near Genoa, along with five other cardinals, by order of the Pope himself. This shocking act of papal retribution sent ripples across Europe, further deepening the schism by alienating many who might otherwise have supported the Roman pontiff. His execution underscored the extreme measures taken by both sides to assert legitimacy and control during an era when the very fabric of the Catholic Church was rent by division."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-23T09:18:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39930605833", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1309, Amelia"}, {"title": "Spiritual seeking and religious community formation", "type": "Idea", "year": "1309, Amelia"}], "newValue": ["The election of Pope Urban VI in 1378 created immediate instability within the College of Cardinals, leading to the Western Schism.", "Pope Urban VI's increasingly autocratic and volatile behavior alienated many cardinals, including Giovanni d'Amelia.", "The ongoing political rivalries among European powers exacerbated the divisions within the Catholic Church during the late 14th century."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-23T09:18:00Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#39930605833", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped religious practice and spiritual communities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1386-01-27"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1386-01-27"}], "newValue": ["Giovanni d'Amelia's execution by Pope Urban VI further intensified the political and religious divisions of the Western Schism.", "His death contributed to the perception of Urban VI as a tyrannical leader, hi